Connect VCC to 3.3V (most modules tolerate 3.3 V–5 V), GND to GND, SDA to GPIO 21, and SCL to GPIO 22. If you prefer different pins, connect SDA/SCL to any GPIOs and initialize the Wire bus with those pins before using the display.

Install the required libraries via the Arduino Library Manager:
- Adafruit_SSD1306
- Adafruit_GFX

Example code:

```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>
#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>

#define SCREEN_WIDTH 128   // OLED width, in pixels
#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64   // OLED height, in pixels
#define OLED_RESET    -1   // Reset pin # (or -1 if sharing Arduino reset pin)
Adafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, OLED_RESET);

void setup() {
  // Use default I2C pins (GPIO21 = SDA, GPIO22 = SCL)
  Wire.begin(21, 22);   // Change numbers if you wired to different pins

  // SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC = generate display voltage from 3.3V internally
  // 0x3C is the most common I2C address; change to 0x3D if your module uses that
  if (!display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, 0x3C)) {
    Serial.println(F("SSD1306 allocation failed"));
    for (;;); // Don't proceed, loop forever
  }

  display.clearDisplay();
  display.setTextSize(1);
  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);
  display.setCursor(0, 0);
  display.println(F("Hello, ESP32!"));
  display.display();
}

void loop() {
  // Add your code here
}
```

If you’re unsure of the module’s I2C address, run an I2C scanner sketch to detect it (common addresses are 0x3C or 0x3D). Most ESP32 boards have internal pull‑ups on the I2C lines, but external 4.7 kΩ resistors to 3.3 V can be added if needed.

